  "history": "Husband is a Norse word in disguise. In Old Norse, húsbóndi was ‘the master of the house’: hús ‘house’ + bóndi, someone who lives on and works the land. Late Old English borrowed it as hūsbonda. At first it meant the head of a household, not a married man; that meaning came around 1300. The farming sense is still in husbandry, the work of keeping animals and growing crops. The word wife is older and English: Old English wīf simply meant ‘woman’. That sense survives in midwife, ‘the woman who is with you’ at a birth. German Weib is related, but today it sounds old-fashioned or rude.",
  "historyEinfach": "Husband is a Viking word. It meant ‘the man of the house’. Later it meant ‘a married man’. Wife is an old English word. It meant ‘woman’. A midwife is a woman who helps when a baby is born.",
